  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 202 PM EDT Sun Oct 25 2026\n\n- Cooler To Start The Week\n\nMid-level water vapor imagery continues to show ridging across the \ncentral Great Lakes region today which is keeping fair conditions \nacross the region. Surface observations upstream show a cold front \nforecast to drift across the region overnight. Frontal passage is \nfavored to be dry, with just clouds across southwest Michigan. Can't \nrule out light showers across Central Lower Michigan though \nprobabilistic guidance keeps chances less than 15 percent. These \nclouds will lower the fog threat across the area, which is well \nreflected in NBM and REFS probabilistic guidance. Post frontal highs \nMonday and Tuesday will be in the low to mid sixties for most.\n\n- Warming Into Midweek With Showers Possible\n\nSoutherly flow behind a warm front arrives late Tuesday bringing \nwarmer conditons mid-week. Highs are favored to rebound to near \nseventy which is notably above average (which is the upper fifties \nthis time of year). Decent signal exists in guidance for showers and \nmaybe isolated thunderstorms given weak elevated instability between \nlate Wednesday and late Thursday.\n\n- Widespread Rain Returns This Weekend\n\nA shortwave associated with the arrival of the more active \nsubtropical jet marks the beginning of more widespread rain chances \nthis weekend. Some question of whether rain impacts Halloween (PoPs \naround 40 percent as of present) or holds off until late \nSaturday/early Sunday. Rain continues into Sunday as well. Ensemble \nguidance has a mean of around 0.5 inches, with 90th percentile (high \nend) amounts of 1.5-2 inches possible in spots. So overall soaking \nrain is expected this upcoming weekend into early next week.",
